**Summary of the 2015 "Journal of Controlled Release" paper:**
This paper investigates Nano-11, a dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticle, as a vaccine adjuvant. It demonstrates that Nano-11 has a positive surface charge, adsorbs protein antigens efficiently, and is readily phagocytized by dendritic cells (DCs). In vitro, Nano-11 activates DCs by increasing costimulatory molecule expression and secreting IL-1β and IL-12p40. In vivo, it significantly enhances antibody responses to two protein antigens. The induced local inflammation is transient and characterized by monocytes. Nano-11 is presented as a safe, effective, and cost-effective adjuvant candidate.

**Summary of the 2017 "npj Vaccines" paper:**
This 2017 paper details the in vivo kinetics and cellular targeting of Nano-11, an α-D-glucan nanoparticle vaccine adjuvant. It shows Nano-11 has comparable or superior adjuvanticity to aluminum hydroxide. After injection, Nano-11 remains localized, clearing gradually over three weeks, and induces a transient inflammatory response primarily involving monocytes/macrophages. Critically, Nano-11 efficiently targets and delivers adsorbed antigens to migratory dendritic cells (MigDCs) in draining lymph nodes, with minimal systemic distribution, supporting its potential as a safe and effective next-generation adjuvant.

